      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "For a nonempty positive sample with both coefficients free, the MLE exists and is unique exactly when the observations are not all equal. With $\\mu$ held and $\\sigma$ free, it exists and is unique unless every $\\log x_i$ equals $\\mu$. With $\\sigma$ held, the free mean has a unique MLE even for constant data. Holding both coefficients leaves only likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "Put $y_i = \\log x_i$. The Jacobian is independent of the coefficients, so maximization is the normal likelihood problem. For each positive $\\sigma$, the unique free mean is $\\bar y$. For a fixed or fitted mean, write $Q = \\sum_i(y_i-\\mu)^2$. The scale likelihood is $-n\\log\\sigma-Q/(2\\sigma^2)$ plus a constant. If $Q>0$, its unique maximum is at $\\sigma^2=Q/n$; if $Q=0$, it is unbounded as $\\sigma\\to0$."
